course: dictionary
lesson: 513
title: Assist: help, and more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Assist is a word for help, but it sounds a little more formal. «The teaching assistant will assist you with your reading.» Here the assistant is someone whose job is to help you. You can also assist in a task, which means you take part in helping with it.
explain: In sport, assist is a noun, not a verb. An assist is a pass that helps a teammate score. «Maya made the assist when she passed to Sam, who scored.» Maya did not score, but she gets the credit because her pass helped.
explain: Look at the word «assisted» in this sentence: «"Assisted living" is an American name for a care home where old people get help with daily life.» Assisted is the past form of assist, so it means was given help. Check by swapping in help: "help living" sounds odd, so read the whole phrase to see that it means a place where people get help.
fact: Assist means to help someone do something.
fact: In sport, an assist is a pass that helps a teammate score.
fact: «Assist with» or «assist in» means to take part in helping with a task.
fact: «Assisted living» means a care home where old people get help with daily life.
fact: Assist is a bit more formal than help, so you will often see it in school letters, signs and sports reports.
exercise: «The teaching assistant will assist you with your reading.» What does assist mean here? | To stop you reading | To write your book for you | *To help you | To watch you from far away
exercise: In a football report: «Maya made the assist when she passed to Sam.» What is an assist? | A foul by a defender | *A pass that helps a teammate score | A goal scored from a corner
exercise: Find and copy the word from this sentence that is the past form of assist: «Shops and officials use soft words as well: "plus-size" clothes, "petite" sizes, "assisted living" and "older adults".» | clothes | *assisted | petite | officials
test:
Which word means to help someone do something? | Assemble | *Assist | Assure | Assault
Which word could replace assist in «The nurse will assist the doctor»? | Ignore | Hide from | *Help | Cancel
exam:
Mia says assist is just a fancy word for help. Is she right, and why might a writer choose it? | *Mostly right, because it means help and sounds more formal | Wrong, because it means to stop someone | Wrong, because it only means a goal in sport | Wrong, because it is only used in America
